'' A DATA BLOCK DECLINES WHOLE OR NOT AT ALL -- a short one would silently shift every
'' later READ onto the wrong datum -- so one item it could not fold took the block, its
'' READs and its RESTOREs with it.
''
'' Three items it could not fold, each measured against fbc's own descriptor:
''
''   const S  = "abc"        { (int16)3, (void*)"abc" }
''   const V1 = wstr( "123" ) { (int16)-32765, (void*)L"123" }
''   @"foo"                  { (int16)-2, (void*)"foo" }
''
'' A STRING CONSTANT could never fold here because ConstOf has no string field at all;
'' the note in this file said a wide datum "does not fold here", and now it does. The
'' WIDENESS comes off the constant's INITIALISER, not off the constant, which carries
'' no type worth asking.
''
'' -2 IS NOT A LENGTH. It is a distinct tag saying the NODE is an ADDRESS, so it cannot
'' go through the 'length or &h8000' path at all. Only a STRING LITERAL operand is
'' accepted: '@somevar' would be a relocation the descriptor cannot express, and
'' guessing it would put a number where an address belongs.
''
'' A POINTER target reads with fb_DataReadULongint -- the runtime moves the ADDRESS and
'' has no pointer reader of its own -- and on win32 with the 32-bit entry point, for the
'' same reason INTEGER splits.
''
'' wstring/data.bas: 10 declines to 0.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --dump-ir tests/mir/src/data-string-const.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: verify: ok
'' EXPECT-OUT: 0 statements not lowered
'' EXPECT-OUT: 3 -> $str0
'' EXPECT-OUT: 32771 -> $wstr1
'' EXPECT-OUT: -2 -> $str2
'' EXPECT-OUT: fb_DataReadULongint
'' GOLDEN: expected/data-string-const.out
